## Docker Setup

The application can be run in both development and production modes using Docker.

### Development Mode

Development mode includes hot-reloading and debugging capabilities:

```bash
# Start development container
./scripts/docker-dev.sh

# Or using docker-compose directly
docker-compose up web-dev
```

The development server will be available at `http://localhost:3000`

Features:
- Hot-reloading
- Source code mounting
- Development dependencies included
- Real-time code updates

### Production Mode

Production mode runs an optimized build:

```bash
# Start production container
./scripts/docker-prod.sh

# Or using docker-compose directly
docker-compose up web-prod
```

The production server will be available at `http://localhost:3000`

Features:
- Optimized build
- Minimal dependencies
- Production-ready configuration

### Making Changes

In development mode, changes to the source code will automatically trigger a rebuild of the affected components. The process works as follows:

1. Edit any source file
2. Next.js will detect the change
3. The application will hot-reload
4. Changes will be visible in the browser

No container rebuild is necessary for code changes in development mode.

### Container Management

Common Docker commands:

```bash
# View logs
docker-compose logs -f web-dev

# Stop containers
docker-compose down

# Rebuild containers
docker-compose build

# Remove volumes
docker-compose down -v
```

### Troubleshooting Docker

1. Port Conflicts
```bash
# Check if port 3000 is in use
lsof -i :3000

# Change port in docker-compose.yml if needed
ports:
  - "3001:3000"  # Maps container port 3000 to host port 3001
```

2. Volume Mounting Issues
```bash
# Verify volume mounts
docker-compose exec web-dev ls -la /app

# Reset volumes
docker-compose down -v
docker-compose up web-dev
```

3. Hot Reload Not Working
```bash
# Check Next.js logs
docker-compose logs -f web-dev

# Restart development container
docker-compose restart web-dev
```

### Environment Variables

Docker environment variables can be set in:
- docker-compose.yml
- .env file (not tracked in git)
- Command line when running containers

Example .env file:
```env
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_HOST=host.docker.internal
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_PORT=8002
```

### Docker Network Configuration

The containers are configured to access the host machine through `host.docker.internal`:

```yaml
extra_hosts:
  - "host.docker.internal:host-gateway"
```

This allows the application to connect to services running on the host machine, such as the recipe analysis service.